  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe coldest air of the season is settling in as rain \ntransitions to snow in the northeast mountains. Central guidance\nhas cut snow accumulations down some but the impacts have not \nchanged. Some accumulations are still possible in the northeast\nmountains to where across elevations higher than 2,500 feet \ncould see a light 1 inch of snow or some localized higher \namounts up to 2 inches along the peaks and ridges through this\nmorning. This could cause slick spots on roadways for the \nmorning commute, however an SPS is issued to cover this impact \nsince advisory criteria is well below at this point. The \nmoisture is relatively shallow and not quite in the dendritic \ngrowth zone, therefore this limits the amount of snowfall that \nis expected now. \n\nUnder the broad upper trough regime light rain will exist until\ntemperatures drop to near freezing across the lowlands, however\ndue to warm surface anything that may transition over to snow \nmixed in with rain will pose no accumulation's across the \nlowlands. The higher elevations may see the transition to all \nsnow outside the northeast mountains, however with ground \ntemperatures too warm accumulations will be on the light side, \nif any. \n\nPrecipitation chances taper off for the lowlands after the \nmorning and through the rest of today. Any hydrometeors that \nfall will be likely be confined to the mountains for the rest of\nFriday and beyond. Little additional accumulations are expected \nafter this morning. As the upper level trough pushes east \nupslope flow will provide additional snow showers to the \nnortheast mountains well past Saturday until medium range models\ncut off activity due to the upslope effect by Sunday afternoon \nas high pressure builds in.\n\nThe area will remain dry with temperatures moderating back to \nnear normal for this time of year by midweek and high pressure \nwill keep the area dry for the rest of the forecast period and \npossibly beyond for the next weekend.",
